{
  "schemaVersion": "1.0",
  "scriptId": "sint-04-doubling-trebling-fraction-principal",
  "sourceType": "MANUAL",
  "metadata": {
    "title": "Doubling, Trebling, and Interest as a Fraction of Principal",
    "description": "Turn amount multiples and principal fractions into fast rate-time relationships without needing the actual principal.",
    "teachingApproach": "Use 'how many principals of interest?' as the mental model. Move from doubling to higher multiples, then to fractional-interest statements and the rate=time quadratic shortcut. Practice should make learners translate words into an interest fraction before touching the formula.",
    "targetDurationMinutes": 30,
    "sourceScope": "R.S. Aggarwal, Quantitative Aptitude for Competitive Examinations, Chapter 22, Simple Interest (printed pp. 641–662). Approved scope: Teach that doubling means S.I.=P, trebling means S.I.=2P, and in general an amount multiple converts to an interest multiple. Cover rate/time shortcuts for doubling and trebling, S.I. given as a fraction or percentage of principal, the pattern where rate percent and time are numerically equal, and comparison of rates from different amount-multiple statements. Emphasise cancelling P before doing arithmetic."
  },
  "startNodeId": "start",
  "nodes": {
    "start": {
      "id": "start",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Doubling, Trebling, and Interest as a Fraction of Principal\n\nTurn amount multiples and principal fractions into fast rate-time relationships without needing the actual principal.\n\nWe will build the idea with small numbers first, then move into the exam-style patterns from the chapter.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1": {
      "id": "teach_1",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "If an amount doubles under simple interest, the extra money equals exactly one principal. So 'doubles in T years' means PRT/100 = P, and P cancels.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1_step"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1_step": {
      "id": "teach_1_step",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "A sum doubling in 20 years means 20R/100 = 1, so R = 5%.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "q_1"
        }
      ]
    },
